  "explanation": {
    "short": "Hindi is written in small blocks: a letter plus a vowel sign. Each letter (प न म र) carries a short \"a\" inside it; the stroke ा after a letter makes it a long \"aa\", and ी makes it a long \"ee\". So पानी is pā-nī, \"water\".",
    "more": "The signs ा and ी are written after the letter they belong to. The little reading under a word, while you are learning, uses a bar over a long vowel: ā is a long \"aa\", ī a long \"ee\". A few more of its letters: c is the \"ch\" of \"church\", ś is \"sh\", and ṁ is the nose sound. Listen to each word, then look at where the strokes sit."
  },
